// Probe 03: does a GUARD (not a refinement) handle a negative literal
// comparison correctly today? Same `expr` grammar as a refinement predicate
// (`guard_expr -> expr`, `refinement -> expr`), and `apply_guard/3` calls the
// SAME `alternatives/1` that `refine/3` calls -- so if the two sites truly
// disagree, the disagreement is not in the shared grammar, it is in
// `comparison/1`'s narrower notion of "constant" plus how the two call sites
// react to `unknown` (refine/3 errors; apply_guard/3 silently credits nothing).
//
// These two clauses partition int exactly at -5: if the guard genuinely
// narrows the residual, the pair is exhaustive and the compiler accepts it
// with no catch-all. If the guard's negative literal is read as `unknown`
// (silently, per apply_guard/3's `{bs_types:none(), Ty}` branch), the second
// clause credits nothing and the compiler reports a missing catch-all
// instead of compiling clean.
module Probe03

public atom Classify(int n)

Classify(n) when n >= -5 -> :in
Classify(n) when n < -5  -> :out
